## Method
- Read `.swarm/U-RUN-SPEC.md` §2b (U7 contract), the task table row (checks
  a-e), ruling U-2026-09-04l (§10), ACCESSIBILITY.md (17 points), and the
  attempt-1 FAIL verdict this attempt claims to fix.
- Read the U7.2 manifest and the diffs of the four claimed files
  (`web/templates/pages/insights.html`, `web/templates/components/shared/
  kpi-tile.html`, `web/static/js/dashboard.js`, `web/static/js/insights.js`)
  against `git diff master`.
- Started `scripts/whatif-verify.sh start 8099` (throwaway data copy) and
  ran INDEPENDENT Playwright probes (not the manifest's numbers alone)
  against the live rendered pages: a Tab-walk/keyboard-activation check on
  every KPI tile, every Insights nav target, and a sample of the sortable
  `<th>` buttons; a full sitewide axe sweep (my own harness, then the
  worker's own `u7_axe_probe.js` re-run byte-for-byte as a cross-check).
- Built an isolated harness (real `kpi-tile.html`/`kpis.html` markup,
  real `dashboard.js`, real `axe-core`) to force-render the Budget tile's
  "no target set" branch — a state the running verify-server's fixture
  data does not reach — and tested keyboard activation of the nested
  `<a href="/whatif">` plus ran axe against that exact markup.
- Stopped the server (`scripts/whatif-verify.sh stop 8099`) when done.

## 1. Keyboard operability — the three families attempt 1 failed — PASS
Independently reproduced live against `localhost:8099` (not just re-running
the manifest's harness):

- **5 dashboard KPI tiles** (`kpi-tile.html` → `kpis.html`): all `DIV
  tabIndex=0 role="button"`, focusable, `aria-label="<Label> details"`.
  Enter on each opens `#kpi-detail-container` (verified: `EnterOpens=true`
  ×5, income/expenses/living/healthcare/budget).
- **12 Insights sortable `<th>` headers**: now `<th scope="col">` wrapping
  a real `<button type="button" data-sort-fn=... data-sort=...>`. Native
  buttons get Enter/Space for free; independently focused and pressed
  Enter on a sample of 4 (recurring-payments table) — `aria-sort` set to
  `ascending` on each, matching click behavior. `scope="col"` retained;
  accessible name is the visible column text ("Description", "Amount",
  etc.) — good.
- **4 Insights navigation targets**: the subscriptions card is a real
  `<a href="/explorer?...">` (native, no JS needed). The 3 `<tr
  data-navigate-href tabindex="0" role="link" aria-label="Open … in
  Explorer">` row families: independently focused row[0] (a
  recurring-payments row, aria-label "Open Amazon in Explorer") and
  pressed Enter — navigated to `/explorer?search=Amazon&type=Outflow`,
  matching click behavior. 26 rendered row instances present in the
  fixture data, all carry the same `tabindex`/`role`/`aria-label` pattern
  by construction (grep-verified across all three markup sites).

No inline `onclick=` reintroduced (`grep -rc 'onclick='` across
`web/templates` = 0).

## 2. Pattern correctness

- **Sortable headers**: correct. `<th scope="col">` kept; button carries
  visible, non-empty accessible name; `aria-sort` tracked by
  `updateSortIcons()` in `insights.js`.
- **Nav rows (`<tr role="link">`)**: correct. A `<tr>` genuinely cannot
  become an `<a>` without losing its `<td>` children; the
  `tabindex="0" role="link"` + scoped keydown (`e.target === el` guard,
  so a keypress inside a nested control is never hijacked) fallback is
  the right choice, and the `aria-label` is meaningful and per-row.
- **KPI tiles — REAL DEFECT.** The Budget tile's "no combined target set"
  branch (`web/templates/components/kpis.html:151-154`) nests a real
  `<a href="/whatif">` inside the tile's outer `<div role="button"
  tabindex="0" data-kpi-detail="expenses">` (`kpi-tile.html:24`). Traced
  both halves of the ruling's own two-part question:
  - *Does the keydown fire the modal even when the inner link is
    focused?* No — verified correct. Built an isolated harness with the
    exact rendered markup (`kpi-tile-open`'s attribute string + the
    "Not set"/`<a href="/whatif">` block from `kpis.html`) plus the real
    `dashboard.js`. Tab from the tile lands on the `<a>`
    (`tag=A href=/whatif`); pressing Enter there navigates to `/whatif`
    (native browser link behavior — `e.target !== el` in the keydown
    handler correctly excludes it), and does NOT open the KPI modal. The
    worker's `e.target === el` guard works as designed.
  - *Does the nesting confuse the accessibility tree?* **Yes.** Ran
    axe-core (wcag2a/wcag2aa/wcag21aa/wcag22aa tags, same ruleset as the
    project's own oracle) against that exact isolated markup:
    `nested-interactive` (impact: serious, tags include `wcag2a`,
    `wcag412` — WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value, Level A, part of the
    "WCAG 2.2 AA" baseline ACCESSIBILITY.md line 3 states) fires on the
    outer `<div role="button">` because it contains a focusable `<a>`
    descendant: "Interactive controls must not be nested — not always
    announced by screen readers or can cause focus problems for
    assistive technologies."
  - **This is NEW this attempt, not carried over from master.**
    Reconstructed master's markup for the same branch
    (`git show master:web/templates/components/kpis.html`, lines
    188-189): the pre-U7 `<div onclick="openKPIDetail('expenses')">` has
    **no `role` attribute**. Ran the same axe ruleset against that
    markup: zero violations — axe's `nested-interactive` rule only fires
    on elements carrying an interactive ARIA role, so master's bare
    `onclick` div (accessibility-broken in its own right, but not via
    this rule) does not trip it. Attempt 2's fix — adding
    `role="button"` to make the tile keyboard-operable — is exactly what
    newly triggers this WCAG 4.1.2 violation on this one branch.
  - **Why no axe sweep caught it:** the "no budget target configured"
    state never renders in the verify-server's fixture data (confirmed:
    `grep -c 'Not set'` on the live `/dashboard` response = 0), so
    neither attempt 1's, nor attempt 2's, nor my own independent sitewide
    sweep (§3 below) ever scanned this branch. It is nonetheless a real,
    commonly-reachable state (any household that hasn't set a What-If
    budget target yet — arguably the FIRST-run state) and the exact
    branch the worker's own manifest cites as the reason a native
    `<button>` wasn't used for the tiles. Same class of gap as the
    U6/U-2026-09-04j hidden-state coverage misses (rulings h/j/k):
    automated coverage depends on fixture data reaching the state; it
    didn't here.

## 3. Full axe sweep — PASS (independently reproduced, not just trusted)
My own first-pass sweep (simpler harness, no tab/modal-opener activation)
surfaced spurious violations on `/whatif` and dark-mode contrast on
several pages; re-running the **worker's own, unmodified**
`u7_axe_probe.js` (which opens every `<details>`, clicks every
tab/quick-adjust/collapse toggle, and opens every KPI/month-detail/
drilldown modal before scanning — the correct methodology per
ruling U-2026-09-03h/U-2026-09-04k) reproduced **18/18 clean** (9 pages ×
light/dark) exactly as claimed. My simpler harness's extra findings were
an artifact of an inferior probe (not opening hidden tabs/panels), not
real defects — discarded per the "necessary but not sufficient, verify
with the right harness" standard, and confirmed by direct comparison.
This satisfies criterion (e)'s sitewide-zero bar and shows attempt 1's
clean axe result did not regress under the tag set the oracle actually
runs. It does NOT catch the nested-interactive gap in §2 because that
defect requires fixture data axe never sees (§2's coverage-gap note).

